    # b[i] will store the number of stones the i-th alien has.
    # Note: The problem uses 1-based indexing for years and aliens.
    # Alien i becomes an adult at year i.
    # Let's use 0-based indexing for the list 'a'.
    # Alien i (0-indexed) becomes an adult at year i+1.
    
    # We need to track how many stones each alien has as they become adults.
    # When alien i (0-indexed) becomes an adult at year i+1:
    # 1. They receive 1 stone from every alien j < i who currently has > 0 stones.
    # 2. They lose 1 stone at every year k > i+1 if they have > 0 stones at that time.
    
    # Let's re-evaluate the process:
    # Year 1: Alien 1 becomes adult. No one was an adult before.
    # Year 2: Alien 2 becomes adult. Alien 1 (now adult) gives 1 stone if A_1 > 0.
    # Year 3: Alien 3 becomes adult. Alien 1 and 2 (now adults) give 1 stone if they have > 0.
    # ...
    # Year k: Alien k becomes adult. Aliens 1 to k-1 (now adults) give 1 stone if they have > 0.
    
    # Let S_k be the number of adults who have at least one stone at year k.
    # At year k:
    # Alien k's stones: B_k = A_k + S_k
    # For all j < k: If B_j > 0, B_j = B_j - 1.
    
    # This looks like we can track the "net change".
    # Let's trace Sample 1: A = [5, 0, 9, 3]
    # Year 1: Alien 1 becomes adult. S_1 = 0. B_1 = 5.
